I do find the query succeeds if I change my sort to:
```
    "sort": [
        {
            "@participant1": {
              "order": "desc"
            },
            "request_id": {
              "order": "desc"
            }
        }
    ]
```

Though it does not work if the order is swapped (`request_id`, then `@participant1`)

> Though it does not work if the order is swapped (request_id, then @participant1)

@reporter that makes sense, as we only look at the primary sort.

@participant4 I don't understand your point. Is there any scenario you have in mind where sorting on a keyword field works against data in frozen tier? I don't see where this could have to do with versions. It seems to me that this has never worked.

As a workaround, you could try setting `pre_filter_shard_size` to a number that's higher than the number of shards your query hits. That may cause more problems than it solves though, as it will effectively disable the can match phase entirely for your query. On the other hand, the can match phase would not work anyways when sorting on a keyword field. In short, Elasticsearch will need to execute the query on all shards, including all of those from the frozen tier. That can easily turn into a very expensive operation, depending on how much data you have in the frozen tier. Ideally, you'd have a time range filter that allows to skip all shards that can't possibly match it without querying them, and not sort by keyword as primary sort.
